<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry IV of Plauen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1510">1510</a>–<a href="/years/1554">1554</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Heinrich IV of Plauen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plauen">Plauen</a> was a member of the Reuss family of Vogtland lords who became Burgrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> and chancellor of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> under Ferdinand I. As a reward for his loyalty in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-war">Schmalkaldic War</a> he recovered Plauen and other Vogtland lands in 1547, briefly restoring his family's lordship before it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-plauen.jpg" alt="Henry IV of Plauen" width="238" height="132" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Glaser · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HeinrichIVHof.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry IV belonged to the elder line of the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plauen">Plauen</a>. He was born in 1510, probably on 24 August, at Hartenštejn Castle, the son of Burgrave Henry III of Plauen and his second wife, Barbara of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a>. After his father's death in 1519 he grew up first in his mother's care and, after her remarriage in 1521, under a guardian she chose, Zdenko Leo Rosenthal, High Burgrave of the Bohemian crown, who brought him to the court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 5 April 1530 King Ferdinand confirmed the Burgraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>, and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> did the same later that year. The Meissen lands themselves had been seized long before by Elector Frederick the Warlike of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>; what Henry valued was the title, its rank and the seat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a> that went with it. In 1532 he married Margaret, Countess of Salm and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg</a>, and held the Bohemian baronies of Toužim, Hartenštejn, Andělská Hora and Prohor, adding Luditz in 1537. On 22 January 1542 Ferdinand made him High Chancellor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Crown of Bohemia</a>.</p><p>Henry helped to conclude the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> of 14 November 1546 between Ferdinand and Duke Maurice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, and he took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muhlberg">Battle of Mühlberg</a> on 24 April 1547 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulation-of-wittenberg">Capitulation of Wittenberg</a> that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-war">Schmalkaldic War</a>. In September 1547 his troops occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greiz">Greiz</a> and drove out the Reuss princes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> made him Imperial Princely Burgrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> on 24 May 1548, and in 1549 Ferdinand named him Lord of Greiz and Gera.</p><p>In 1551 he summoned the Vogtland estates to a diet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleiz">Schleiz</a>, set a governor over them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plauen">Plauen</a> and issued regulations for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greiz">Greiz</a>. Though a Catholic all his life, he resisted every attempt to undo the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> among his Vogtland lords, promoted the Protestant Church and in 1552 adopted a church constitution drawn up by the Protestant superintendent of Plauen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1552 he prepared the meeting of Maurice and Ferdinand at Linz that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-passau">Peace of Passau</a>, which he persuaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> to sign. After Maurice was mortally wounded at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sievershausen">Battle of Sievershausen</a> in July 1553, Henry led the allies against Margrave Albert Alcibiades of Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulmbach">Kulmbach</a>, taking Hof and then besieging the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plassenburg">Plassenburg</a>. He died at Stadtsteinach during the siege on 19 May 1554 and was buried in St John's Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plauen">Plauen</a>. His two sons with Margaret, Henry V and Henry VI, both bore the title of Burgrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry IV of Plauen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Henry IV of Plauen</b>, Burgrave of Meissen and Bohemian chancellor, recovered Plauen in 1547.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-plauen">Lordship of Plauen</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hartenštejn Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. Died in Stadtsteinach, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.14,13.04&amp;year=1538" aria-label="Hartenštejn Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4848 420 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Henry IV of Plauen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5298 690h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5298 690h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.14,13.04&amp;year=1538">What was here in 1538? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
